    {
      "filename": "Sea of Jehovah's Witnesses' Cars (6086229980).jpg",
      "title": "File:Sea of Jehovah's Witnesses' Cars (6086229980).jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b9/Sea_of_Jehovah%27s_Witnesses%27_Cars_%286086229980%29.jpg",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sea_of_Jehovah%27s_Witnesses%27_Cars_(6086229980).jpg",
      "width": 1003,
      "height": 1003,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 2.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0",
      "artist": "Tom Parnell from Scottish Borders, Scotland",
      "description": "On approaching the castle we were a little concerned that Historic Scotland's recent Huntingtower/Elcho promotion had been a little bit too succesful!  As it turned out we had the castle to ourselves...\nUpon leaving, we realised who all these cars belonged to...\nUnfortunately we left about the same time as thousands of Jehovah's Witnesses leaving the nearby football stadium, and the ensuing traffic jam had already appeared.  This was just the overflow car park.\n\nPerhaps it was just my unease at seeing hordes of smartly-dressed people departing a football stadium, knowing who they were and the organisation they belonged to (I'm trying very hard to be polite and resist using the word 'cult') but I couldn't get Roald Dahl's The Witches out of my head... You know, the bit where the doors are all locked, sightlines blocked, and they remove their smart hats and wigs, revealing their true selves underneath...",
      "dateCreated": "2011-07-17 15:34",
      "sourceType": "commons-category",
      "sourceArticleId": "huntingtower-castle-wp",
      "sourceGeohash4": "gfj8"
    }
  ],
